# C++ Study List for Hedge Fund Interviews

Ordered by priority. Start at the top.

## Tier 1 — Must know (asked constantly)
- [ ] RAII (resource acquisition is initialization)
- [ ] Smart pointers: unique_ptr, shared_ptr, weak_ptr (when to use each)
- [ ] Move semantics: rvalue refs, std::move, std::forward
- [ ] Rule of 0/3/5
- [ ] const correctness: const, constexpr, const methods
- [ ] References vs pointers vs values (when to pass each)
- [ ] Stack vs heap allocation
- [ ] STL containers + their complexity (vector, unordered_map, map, set, deque)

## Tier 2 — Strongly expected
- [ ] Iterators and categories (input, forward, bidirectional, random access)
- [ ] STL algorithms (sort, find, lower_bound, accumulate, transform)
- [ ] Which container to pick + trade-offs (speed vs memory)
- [ ] emplace vs insert, reserve, capacity
- [ ] Memory bugs: leaks, dangling pointers, double free
- [ ] new/delete vs malloc/free
- [ ] Lambdas (capture by value vs reference)
- [ ] auto, range-based for, structured bindings

## Tier 3 — HFT-specific (Citadel, Jane Street dig here)
- [ ] Cache locality, cache lines, false sharing
- [ ] Branch prediction
- [ ] Copy elision / RVO (return value optimization)
- [ ] Inlining, what -O2/-O3 do
- [ ] Memory layout: alignment, padding
- [ ] Concurrency: threads, mutex, lock_guard, condition variables
- [ ] Atomics and memory ordering
- [ ] Race conditions, deadlocks

## Tier 4 — Advanced / nice to have
- [ ] Templates: function, class, specialization
- [ ] Variadic templates
- [ ] Type traits, SFINAE
- [ ] std::optional, std::variant
- [ ] Lock-free programming
- [ ] Placement new
- [ ] Concepts (C++20)

---

## If you only do 6 things
RAII → smart pointers → move semantics → rule of 5 → const → references vs pointers.
These are guaranteed to come up.

## Main resource
"Effective Modern C++" by Scott Meyers — covers most of Tier 1 and 2.
